1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,665
Watch the valve on a bicycle tyre.

2
00:00:02,665 --> 00:00:05,309
Ride forward, and it goes round one way.

3
00:00:05,309 --> 00:00:09,057
Push the bicycle backwards, and
it goes round the other way.

4
00:00:09,457 --> 00:00:12,437
Both are turns, but they
are not the same turn.

5
00:00:12,887 --> 00:00:17,371
Aarav noticed this while wheeling his
bicycle back out of the college stand.

6
00:00:17,721 --> 00:00:20,936
How can one number tell us
which way the valve turned?

7
00:00:21,436 --> 00:00:22,438
With a sign.

8
00:00:22,438 --> 00:00:26,795
Today we meet positive and negative
angles, on a circle called the unit

9
00:00:26,795 --> 00:00:27,695
circle.

10
00:00:28,087 --> 00:00:31,431
First, the stage for every
angle in this chapter.

11
00:00:31,431 --> 00:00:33,144
Draw the x and y axes.

12
00:00:33,144 --> 00:00:37,329
Put a circle with its centre at
the origin O, and radius one.

13
00:00:37,329 --> 00:00:39,082
This is the unit circle.

14
00:00:39,482 --> 00:00:43,870
The point A, at one on the x
axis, is where every angle starts.

15
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:46,173
The ray OA is the initial side.

16
00:00:46,573 --> 00:00:49,547
Turn the ray, and it ends
somewhere on the circle.

17
00:00:49,547 --> 00:00:51,367
That ray is the terminal side.

18
00:00:52,017 --> 00:00:53,351
Now the direction.

19
00:00:53,351 --> 00:00:58,145
If the ray turns anticlockwise, against
the way clock hands move, the angle

20
00:00:58,145 --> 00:00:59,117
is positive.

21
00:00:59,517 --> 00:01:04,173
If it turns clockwise, the same way
as clock hands, the angle is negative.

22
00:01:04,573 --> 00:01:05,768
Side by side.

23
00:01:05,768 --> 00:01:09,230
Anticlockwise to the top
is plus ninety degrees.

24
00:01:09,230 --> 00:01:12,693
Clockwise to the bottom
is minus ninety degrees.

25
00:01:13,293 --> 00:01:14,268
Now you try.

26
00:01:14,268 --> 00:01:18,657
Where does minus two hundred and
seventy degrees end: at the top, or at

27
00:01:18,657 --> 00:01:19,557
the bottom?

28
00:01:19,527 --> 00:01:20,690
Pause and think.

29
00:01:23,890 --> 00:01:24,880
At the top!

30
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:28,557
Three quarter turns clockwise
go down, left, then up.

31
00:01:28,557 --> 00:01:31,203
It ends exactly where plus ninety ends.

32
00:01:31,853 --> 00:01:35,110
The two axes cut the
plane into four quadrants.

33
00:01:35,110 --> 00:01:40,069
We number them one to four, going
anticlockwise from the positive x axis.

34
00:01:40,469 --> 00:01:44,474
An angle belongs to the quadrant
where its terminal side lands.

35
00:01:44,474 --> 00:01:46,703
Sixty degrees lands in the first.

36
00:01:46,703 --> 00:01:49,494
One hundred and fifty lands in the second.

37
00:01:49,894 --> 00:01:51,934
And the ray can keep turning.

38
00:01:51,934 --> 00:01:56,261
Four hundred and twenty degrees
is one full turn, then sixty more.

39
00:01:56,261 --> 00:01:59,054
It lands exactly where
sixty degrees lands.

40
00:01:59,704 --> 00:02:01,973
Now a beautiful idea from NCERT.

41
00:02:01,973 --> 00:02:04,406
Every real number can be an angle.

42
00:02:04,756 --> 00:02:08,724
Stand a number line upright, so
that it touches the circle at A.

43
00:02:08,724 --> 00:02:09,860
Zero sits on A.

44
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:14,825
Wrap the positive numbers around the
circle anticlockwise, and the negative

45
00:02:14,825 --> 00:02:16,096
numbers clockwise.

46
00:02:16,496 --> 00:02:18,163
Watch the number one land.

47
00:02:18,163 --> 00:02:22,262
The arc from A has length one, so
it marks an angle of one radian.

48
00:02:22,662 --> 00:02:25,886
Every real number lands
on exactly one point.

49
00:02:25,886 --> 00:02:29,078
Two pi lands back on A after one full lap.

50
00:02:29,078 --> 00:02:31,697
Minus one lands one radian below A.

51
00:02:32,347 --> 00:02:37,136
So radian measures and real numbers
can be treated as one and the same.

52
00:02:37,786 --> 00:02:40,688
So when do two angles
end at the same point?

53
00:02:40,688 --> 00:02:41,954
Three small steps.

54
00:02:42,304 --> 00:02:43,204
Step one.

55
00:02:43,085 --> 00:02:47,515
A turn of two pi takes the ray once
round, back to where it started.

56
00:02:48,265 --> 00:02:49,165
Step two.

57
00:02:49,048 --> 00:02:52,753
A turn of minus two pi takes
it once round the other way.

58
00:02:52,753 --> 00:02:54,567
Again, back to the start.

59
00:02:55,317 --> 00:02:56,261
Step three.

60
00:02:56,261 --> 00:03:01,453
So adding any whole number of turns,
two n pi, where n is an integer, never

61
00:03:01,453 --> 00:03:03,084
changes the end point.

62
00:03:03,834 --> 00:03:08,700
Theta and two n pi plus theta always
end at the same point on the unit

63
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:09,600
circle.

64
00:03:10,062 --> 00:03:11,142
Remember this.

65
00:03:11,142 --> 00:03:16,031
Anticlockwise is plus, clockwise is
minus, and a full turn of two pi brings

66
00:03:16,031 --> 00:03:16,931
you back.

67
00:03:17,559 --> 00:03:18,459
Quick one.

68
00:03:18,421 --> 00:03:23,010
Does minus three hundred degrees end
at the same point as sixty degrees?

69
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:24,229
Pause and think.

70
00:03:27,429 --> 00:03:28,329
Yes!

71
00:03:27,959 --> 00:03:32,867
The difference is three hundred and
sixty degrees, exactly one full turn.

72
00:03:33,517 --> 00:03:35,252
Now, one worked example.

73
00:03:35,602 --> 00:03:38,267
Where does minus one thousand degrees end?

74
00:03:38,267 --> 00:03:42,635
Find its quadrant, and the angle from
zero to three sixty with the same

75
00:03:42,635 --> 00:03:43,535
end point.

76
00:03:43,972 --> 00:03:44,872
Step one.

77
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:47,809
It is negative, so the
ray turns clockwise.

78
00:03:48,559 --> 00:03:49,459
Step two.

79
00:03:49,417 --> 00:03:54,221
Full turns do not change the end
point, so keep adding three sixty until

80
00:03:54,221 --> 00:03:57,183
the angle is between zero and three sixty.

81
00:03:57,933 --> 00:03:58,899
Step three.

82
00:03:58,899 --> 00:04:03,631
Minus one thousand, plus three times
three sixty, is eighty degrees.

83
00:04:04,381 --> 00:04:05,281
Step four.

84
00:04:05,259 --> 00:04:10,245
Eighty is between zero and ninety, so
the angle ends in the first quadrant.

85
00:04:10,995 --> 00:04:15,596
Two full turns backwards, then two
hundred and eighty more: the valve stops

86
00:04:15,596 --> 00:04:17,761
exactly where eighty degrees ends.

87
00:04:18,411 --> 00:04:19,876
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:04:20,226 --> 00:04:25,109
Many students think five pi by four
ends at the same point as pi by four,

89
00:04:25,109 --> 00:04:27,006
because they differ by pi.

90
00:04:27,406 --> 00:04:31,573
But pi is only half a turn, which
sends you to the opposite side.

91
00:04:31,573 --> 00:04:34,529
Only a difference of two
n pi brings you back.

92
00:04:35,179 --> 00:04:36,716
Where is this asked?

93
00:04:36,716 --> 00:04:38,150
Rarely on its own.

94
00:04:38,150 --> 00:04:42,966
It sits inside many trigonometry
questions: find the quadrant of an angle,

95
00:04:42,966 --> 00:04:46,968
or the angle from zero to two
pi with the same end point.

96
00:04:47,568 --> 00:04:48,794
Exam level now.

97
00:04:48,794 --> 00:04:53,470
How many of these five angles end
at the same point as pi by three?

98
00:04:53,470 --> 00:04:54,749
Pause and solve.

99
00:05:00,149 --> 00:05:01,325
The answer is C.

100
00:05:01,325 --> 00:05:02,225
Three.

101
00:05:02,381 --> 00:05:04,793
Subtract pi by three from each angle.

102
00:05:04,793 --> 00:05:09,330
The end points match only when the
difference is a whole number of turns.

103
00:05:09,830 --> 00:05:12,395
Seven pi by three gives two pi.

104
00:05:12,395 --> 00:05:13,295
Yes.

105
00:05:12,985 --> 00:05:16,324
Minus five pi by three gives minus two pi.

106
00:05:16,324 --> 00:05:17,224
Yes.

107
00:05:16,914 --> 00:05:19,710
Thirteen pi by three gives four pi.

108
00:05:19,710 --> 00:05:20,610
Yes.

109
00:05:20,799 --> 00:05:23,809
Four pi by three gives pi, half a turn.

110
00:05:23,809 --> 00:05:27,147
Minus pi by three gives
minus two pi by three.

111
00:05:27,147 --> 00:05:28,726
So only three match.

112
00:05:29,326 --> 00:05:30,787
Here is your recap.

113
00:05:30,787 --> 00:05:32,289
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:35,489 --> 00:05:38,487
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:05:38,887 --> 00:05:39,787
One.

116
00:05:39,436 --> 00:05:43,016
Is an angle measured clockwise
positive or negative?

117
00:05:43,916 --> 00:05:44,816
Two.

118
00:05:44,426 --> 00:05:47,608
In which quadrant does
seven hundred degrees end?

119
00:05:48,508 --> 00:05:49,408
Three.

120
00:05:49,121 --> 00:05:52,694
Which real number lands back
on A after one full lap?

121
00:05:53,794 --> 00:05:56,797
Next time: how high is
the valve above the hub?

122
00:05:56,797 --> 00:05:58,762
That height has a name: sine.
